{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Is it good or is it bad?</b> — Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 13:18): if the land is good, that its air is good and its waters are good. And he turned back and said, \"Is it fat?\", to produce wheat and many fruits. But the correct interpretation is that <b>is it good</b> includes all the good qualities and <b>or bad</b> all the bad qualities in their entirety, except that it is possible for the good land to be fat and its fruits fat and that fat things are found in it — pine nuts and oil and dates and figs and the like — or it may be good but lean, requiring constant rains and needing much absence and fertilization, and its fruits may tend toward dryness even though they are good and last longer than the fat ones, and there the lean fruits will be found in great abundance, such as nuts and almonds and apples and carobs and forest fruits. And so it may include whether it is fat land like the plain or lean land like the mountains. But Onkelos translated \"הֲעַתִּירָא הִיא אִם מִסְכֵּנָא\" (Is it rich or poor), for there are lands whose inhabitants are wealthy because of the cheapness found among them and their neighbors are their merchants for all the good found in them, and there are lands that depend on their settlements and their inhabitants are short of hand and yet the lands are good, not bad at all. And the sense of <b>And you shall strengthen yourselves and take</b> (Numbers 13:20) — that they should not be afraid when taking of the fruit of the land, lest they be recognized as spies."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra's paraphrased remark 'Is it fat?' corresponds to his comment on Numbers 13:20 (השמנה היא), which Ramban cites here to show that Ibn Ezra himself reverts to the criterion of fatness."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:50.837376658Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}